---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CTABanner from '../components/CTABanner.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="About 1404 — A transformational technology partner"
  description="1404 is a technology partner for owner-operated companies. Forward-deployed engineers who build and run an AI strategy alongside your team. Founded in Detroit."
>
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pt-40 pb-24 md:pb-32">

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="mb-20">
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl text-text-primary leading-tight">
        Why 1404 Exists
      </h1>
    </header>

    <!-- The Motive -->
    <div class="mb-20">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-6">The Motive</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Growing up in Detroit, you see the pattern. Manufacturing floors that run on tribal knowledge. Construction firms where the most critical processes live in someone's head, or worse, in a spreadsheet no one else understands. Landscaping companies, auto repair chains, dental practices. Companies doing $5M, $20M, $100M in revenue with technology that belongs in 2008.
        </p>
        <p>
          It isn't laziness or ignorance. It's a market failure. The people who build technology don't want to work in these industries, and the people who run these companies can't find them. The bridge between those two worlds doesn't exist.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tools are here now, and most companies still can't get the return. MIT's 2025 study found that about 95% of enterprise AI efforts never move the bottom line. Not because the technology is weak, but because a stack of tools is not a strategy. Closing that gap takes someone who builds the strategy around how your business actually runs, and stays to run it with you. That is why 1404 exists.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- The Mechanism -->
    <div class="mb-20">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-6">The Mechanism</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          1404 works in two parts. It starts with a Foundation. We come on-site, put the best AI tools in your team's hands and get them fluent, connect the systems you already run so they work as one, and build a clear plan from your growth goals.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then it becomes an ongoing Partnership on a flat monthly retainer. We keep building whatever moves the business forward, from AI agents that handle the busywork to custom software when off-the-shelf tools fall short, and we share in the upside we create. We're forward-deployed, working alongside your team.
        </p>
        <p>
          We ramp onto your business in weeks, not the year a new internal hire would spend, because we already have the architecture patterns and industry knowledge built.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Why It Matters -->
    <div class="mb-20">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-6">Why It Matters</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Give one person a shovel and another person an excavator. Come back in a week. The difference between them isn't two or ten times, it's a different category of output entirely. That's what technology does to operations. The company with real-time labor forecasting doesn't just schedule a bit better than the company using spreadsheets. It sees labor problems a week out, while the other company finds out on payday. The company whose systems flag issues the moment they appear doesn't just catch problems faster, it prevents ones the other company never sees coming.
        </p>
        <p>
          And the advantage builds on itself. Every month the system learns more. Every quarter the institutional knowledge deepens. After two years, it knows things about your operations that no single person in your company knows. After five years, the gap between you and the company that didn't invest is hard to close, because the system keeps its knowledge instead of resetting every time someone leaves.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Backed by TCG -->
    <div class="border-t border-border pt-16 mb-20">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-6">Backed by The Construct Group</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          We built our systems and agent architecture inside The Construct Group, a national retail fixture installation company, where we built the entire technology stack from scratch. A platform of more than 180,000 lines, AI agents running daily operations, and ongoing technology leadership. The results led TCG to come on as an equity partner.
        </p>
        <p>
          That means something. TCG put capital behind what we built, after 1404 had been pressure-tested inside a live operating company with real revenue, employees, and operational complexity, before we ever took on a second client. The systems we deploy and the process we follow were developed in production, not in a lab.
        </p>
        <p>
          TCG still runs on 1404's platform, and Ben Barnes, TCG's CEO, actively introduces 1404 to other operators in his network. Since then we've expanded beyond construction into landscaping, auto repair, and financial services. The underlying approach works across industries because the operational problems are structurally similar even when the specifics differ.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Cofounders -->
    <div class="border-t border-border pt-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-10">Cofounders</h2>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl md:text-2xl text-text-primary mb-1">Holden Bryce</h3>
          <p class="text-xs tracking-widest uppercase text-text-muted mb-3">Cofounder</p>
          <a href="mailto:holden@1404.io" class="text-copper text-sm hover:text-copper-bright transition-colors duration-200">holden@1404.io</a>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl md:text-2xl text-text-primary mb-1">Arthur Skatoff</h3>
          <p class="text-xs tracking-widest uppercase text-text-muted mb-3">Cofounder</p>
          <a href="mailto:arthur@1404.io" class="text-copper text-sm hover:text-copper-bright transition-colors duration-200">arthur@1404.io</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABanner />
</PageLayout>
